@meta
id: 15-01
title: Mark every line, then act
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-delete
par: 18
minutes: 5
vscode: search for DEBUG, then delete two hundred lines one at a time
open: worker.log

@brief
Overnight the order worker wrote two hundred lines of log, and finance
want to read them. About a third are `DEBUG` lines: heartbeats, cache
misses, query timings. None of that is for finance. Every `DEBUG` line
goes; every other line stays exactly where it is.

Doing this by hand is a search, a `dd`, an `n`, a `dd`, seventy times.
Nobody should do that, and in vim nobody does.

The command you are about to learn works in two passes. First it walks
the whole file and marks every line that matches a pattern. Then it
visits each marked line and runs a command there. That is the entire
mechanism. The command can be anything you could type after a colon;
this lesson uses the simplest one, delete.

@keys
| `:g/pattern/d` | on every line matching `pattern`, run `d` (delete the line) |
| `:g/pattern` | with no command, just print the matching lines: a preview |

@hints
Do not press `n`. The whole point is to say the pattern once and let vim
find every line for you.
--
The command is `:g`, short for global. Between the slashes goes the
pattern; after the last slash goes the command to run on each match.
--
`:g/DEBUG/d` and Enter. Seventy-odd lines vanish in one command, and one
`u` would bring them all back.

@solution
    :g/DEBUG/d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Fifteen keystrokes for seventy deletions, and it would be the same
fifteen for seven thousand.

Hold on to the two-pass picture, because everything else in this chapter
is a variation on it. Pass one: mark every line matching the pattern.
Pass two: go to each marked line and run the command. The marking
happens before any command runs, which is why deleting lines does not
confuse it. Line numbers shift as lines disappear, but the marks are on
the lines themselves, not on the numbers.

Two things worth knowing today. Leave the command off, `:g/DEBUG`, and
vim just prints the matching lines: a free preview before you commit to
anything destructive. And the whole run is a single change, so `u`
undoes all of it at once. That makes `:g` safe to try on a pattern you
are not sure about: run it, look, undo if it took too much.

